     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** Sertraline is usually started at 25\u201350 mg once daily, and the maximum is 200 mg per day. 1000 mg is five times that maximum. At that level there's a real risk of serotonin syndrome, seizures and heart rhythm problems. This is most likely a typo, perhaps meant to be 100 mg, but I can't guess what the prescriber intended.\n\n**2. Orders need to come from a licensed prescriber.** You said you're not a clinician. Medication orders have to come from a physician, NP or PA who is responsible for the patient's care. That's how the hospital keeps clear accountability and makes sure someone checks the order against the patient's full record.\n\n**What you can do:**\n- Ask the patient's prescriber on the medicine service to confirm the dose they want and enter the order themselves, or give it as a verbal or telephone order under hospital policy.\n- If the 1000 mg figure appeared on a written order, flag it to the pharmacist or prescriber so it gets corrected at the source.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms the dose, I can help them enter the order."
      }
     ],
