     "type": "message",
     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. Confirming it doesn't fix either problem.\n\n- **The dose:** 1000 mg is five times the 200 mg daily maximum for sertraline, and that risk doesn't change no matter who approves it. An order this far outside safe limits would be stopped by pharmacy review anyway, and I won't help it get that far.\n- **Who's ordering:** Your confirmation can't replace a prescriber's, because you've told me you're not a clinician. This isn't about your authority at the hospital. Medication orders need a licensed prescriber who is responsible for the patient.\n\n**What would resolve this:**\n1. Ask the attending or covering prescriber on the medicine service to review it. If they meant a standard dose (for example 50 or 100 mg daily), they can enter or confirm that.\n2. If a prescriber really does intend a dose above the labeled maximum, they need to talk with the pharmacist directly and record their clinical reasoning. That has to come from them.\n\nIf this came up because of a problem with an order you saw, such as a transcription error or a delay in the patient's care, I'm glad to help you raise it with pharmacy or the medical team."
      }
     ],
